1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,316
Why did Russia sell this giant piece

2
00:00:02,316 --> 00:00:04,245
of land for two cents an acre?

3
00:00:04,595 --> 00:00:07,881
In 1867, Russia sold Alaska to the

4
00:00:07,881 --> 00:00:12,230
United States for 7.2 million dollars. That's

5
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:15,322
land more than twice the size of

6
00:00:15,322 --> 00:00:15,902
Texas.

7
00:00:16,252 --> 00:00:18,551
So why sell? Russia was still in

8
00:00:18,551 --> 00:00:21,496
debt after losing the Crimean War. Alaska

9
00:00:21,496 --> 00:00:23,867
was far away, hard to defend, and

10
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:26,741
Russia feared Britain could take it from

11
00:00:26,741 --> 00:00:28,177
next door in Canada.

12
00:00:28,527 --> 00:00:31,435
Back in America, newspapers mocked it as

13
00:00:31,435 --> 00:00:34,925
Seward's Folly, after Secretary of State William

14
00:00:34,925 --> 00:00:37,978
Seward. They called Alaska a giant icebox.

15
00:00:38,328 --> 00:00:41,114
Then came gold. The Klondike Gold Rush

16
00:00:41,114 --> 00:00:44,267
sent tens of thousands of prospectors north

17
00:00:44,267 --> 00:00:47,126
through Alaska, and soon gold was found

18
00:00:47,126 --> 00:00:49,912
in Alaska itself, in places like Nome.

19
00:00:50,262 --> 00:00:53,899
Alaska became the 49th state in 1959.

20
00:00:53,899 --> 00:00:57,240
And in 1968, the biggest oil field

21
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:00,877
in North America was found at Prudhoe

22
00:01:00,877 --> 00:01:01,270
Bay.

23
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:03,997
That oil has been worth many, many

24
00:01:03,997 --> 00:01:06,514
times what America paid. Not bad for

25
00:01:06,514 --> 00:01:08,682
an icebox. Was it the best deal

26
00:01:08,682 --> 00:01:11,199
in history? Tell me in the comments.

